@page "/docs/common/session-recovery"

@* Session recovery: an interactive server session (circuit) holds the page in server memory and
   ends with a lost connection, a restart or memory pressure. Pausing and resuming it: what IzzyBlazor components keep,
   what the app persists, the reconnect dialog, and .NET 11's pause in a hidden tab. Each claim
   was checked on .NET 11 (Nov 10, 2026) with Blazor.pauseCircuit/resumeCircuit and AddAutoPause. *@

<ArticlePage Section="common" Slug="session-recovery">
    <Lead>
        An interactive server page lives in a session on the server, which ends with a lost connection, a restart or a
        server short of memory, and takes the user's work with it. Blazor can pause the session instead and resume it later,
        bringing back what's in the URL or marked <Code>[PersistentState]</Code>.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <TextBlock>
            A pause saves the page's state in the browser and frees the server's memory. Resuming starts a new session from
            that state and builds every component again.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">What comes back</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Table and DataGrid sort, filter, search and page.</strong> They're in the URL, so a resumed page shows
                the same rows. A grid you bind, such as with <Code>@@bind-Page</Code>, keeps what your property keeps.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Anything you bind and mark <Code>[PersistentState]</Code>.</strong> The components rebuild from it: a
                Dialog bound with <Code>@@bind-Open</Code> opens again, a TextField and a RichTextEditor show their text, a grid
                shows its page.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Not what you leave unmarked.</strong> Bound values without <Code>[PersistentState]</Code> start over, as
                after a reload. So does a file chosen in a FileUpload: the browser can't put it back.
            </ListItem>
        </List>
        <Code Block Language="razor" Title="Orders.razor" HighlightLines="13,14,15">@Persisted</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            A persisted value has to serialize to JSON. Keep it to what the user would miss: what they typed, chose or opened.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">The reconnect dialog</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            With a <Anchor Href="docs/components/reconnect-dialog">ReconnectDialog</Anchor> in <Code>App.razor</Code>, a paused
            session shows "Session paused" with a Resume button, which starts the new session. The message says whether the
            server paused it or the page did. A session that can't resume offers Reload.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Pause a hidden tab <Badge Accent="Accent.Primary" Size="BadgeSize.Sm">.NET 11+</Badge></Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            .NET 11 can pause a session by itself once its tab has been out of view for a while, and resume it when the tab
            comes back. Add the <Code>Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Server.AutoPause</Code> package, then turn it on where
            the app maps its components:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="csharp" Title="Program.cs">@AutoPause</Code>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                It doesn't pause while a text field or a RichTextEditor has focus, so text the user is still typing isn't
                lost.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                On phones, the browser stops a backgrounded page's script within seconds, often before the delay runs out.
                The session then ends as a lost connection and comes back through the reconnect dialog.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>A paused tab that comes back resumes by itself; the dialog shows only while it does.</ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Pause from your own code</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>Blazor.pauseCircuit()</Code> and <Code>Blazor.resumeCircuit()</Code> pause and resume from the page's script,
            for a policy of your own, such as pausing after a time without input. On .NET 11 the server can ask a page to pause
            too, before a deployment or restart. See Microsoft's
            <Anchor Href="https://learn.microsoft.com/aspnet/core/blazor/state-management/server#pause-and-resume-circuits" External>pause and resume circuits</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Next steps</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">Render modes</Anchor>: what components do on static and interactive pages.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/reconnect-dialog">ReconnectDialog</Anchor>: the dialog's states and text.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private const string Persisted = """
        @page "/orders"
        @rendermode InteractiveServer

        <DataGrid Items="Orders" @bind-Page="Page" PageSize="20">
            ...
        </DataGrid>

        <Dialog Title="Notes" @bind-Open="NotesOpen">
            <RichTextEditor Label="Notes" @bind-Value="Notes" />
        </Dialog>

        @code {
            [PersistentState] public int Page { get; set; } = 1;
            [PersistentState] public bool NotesOpen { get; set; }
            [PersistentState] public string? Notes { get; set; }
        }
        """;

    private const string AutoPause = """
        using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components;   // AddAutoPause

        app.MapRazorComponents<App>()
            .AddInteractiveServerRenderMode()
            .WithBrowserOptions(options =>
                options.AddAutoPause(pause => pause.HiddenDelay = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(2)));
        """;
}
